<article class="env-lemma" id="0BZR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BZR">Lemma <span data-tag="0BZR">47.24.5</span>.</a> Let $R \to R'$ be a homomorphism of Noetherian rings. Let $\varphi : R \to A$ be a perfect ring map (More on Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/067H" data-tag="067H">15.84.1</a>) such that $R'$ and $A$ are tor independent over $R$. Let $\varphi ' : R' \to A' = A \otimes _ R R'$ be the map induced by $\varphi $. Then we have a functorial isomorphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \varphi ^!(K) \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} A' = (\varphi ')^!(K \otimes _ R^\mathbf {L} R')  \]
</div>
  <p> for $K$ in $D(R)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We may choose a factorization $R \to P \to A$ where $P$ is a polynomial ring over $R$ such that $A$ is a perfect $P$-module, see More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/068Y" data-tag="068Y">15.84.2</a>. This gives a corresponding factorization $R' \to P' \to A'$ by base change. Since we have $(K \otimes _ R^\mathbf {L} P) \otimes _ P^\mathbf {L} P' = (K \otimes _ R^\mathbf {L} R') \otimes _{R'}^\mathbf {L} P'$ by More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/08YU" data-tag="08YU">15.61.5</a> it suffices to construct maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (A, K \otimes _ R^\mathbf {L} P[n]) \otimes _ A^\mathbf {L} A' \longrightarrow R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (A', (K \otimes _ R^\mathbf {L} P[n]) \otimes _ P^\mathbf {L} P')  \]
</div>
    <p>
       functorial in $K$. We have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  A \otimes _ P^\mathbf {L} P' = A \otimes _ R^\mathbf {L} R' = A'  \]
</div>
    <p>
       The first equality by More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0661" data-tag="0661">15.62.2</a> applied to $R, R', P, P'$. The second equality because $A$ and $R'$ are tor independent over $R$. Hence $A$ and $P'$ are tor independent over $P$ and we can use the map (<a href="/tag/0E29" data-tag="0E29">47.14.0.1</a>) constructed in Section <a href="/tag/0E28" data-tag="0E28">47.14</a> for $P, A, P', A'$ get the desired arrow. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0BZP" data-tag="0BZP">47.14.3</a> to finish the proof it suffices to prove that $A$ is a perfect $P$-module which we saw above. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>